<h1>TActiveLinkButton</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveLinkButton" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TActiveLinkButton</tt> is the active control counterpart of
<a href="?page=Controls.LinkButton">TLinkButton</a>. Clicking it starts a callback rather
than a postback. The <tt>OnCallback</tt> event is raised during a callback, after the
<tt>OnClick</tt> event.
</p>

<h2>What updates during a callback</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Effect on the browser</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Text</tt></td>
	<td>Replaces the content of the link.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Enabled</tt></td>
	<td>Set to false, it removes the <tt>href</tt> attribute, so the link stops responding to clicks. Set back to true, it restores the attribute and registers the client script again.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
Both updates are subject to <a href="?page=ActiveControls.Introduction#ClientSideUpdates">the five conditions for a client-side update</a>.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Because the control reaches the server through its <tt>href</tt>, a link button needs
client script to work at all. It is one of the controls with no postback fallback, listed
in the abilities table on the <a href="?page=ActiveControls.Home">Active Controls</a>
page, so a browser without JavaScript cannot activate it. Use
<a href="?page=ActiveControls.ActiveButton">TActiveButton</a> where that matters.
</p>

